    "\n\nThe Echidna finding exposed a real accounting gap in `RWAStaking`. Here's the breakdown:\n\n### The Property That Failed\n\n```solidity\n// Original property\nfunction echidna_staking_solvent() public view returns (bool) {\n    return token.balanceOf(address(staking)) >= staking.totalStaked();\n}\n```\n\nThis asserts: \"the staking contract always holds enough tokens to cover all staked principal.\"\n\n### The Attack Sequence Echidna Found\n\n```\n1. stake(100k tokens, tier 0)     → totalStaked = 100k, balance = 2.1M (pre-funded)\n2. [wait 1+ day]                  → stake becomes eligible\n3. stake(38k tokens, tier 0)      → totalStaked = 138k, balance = 2.138M\n4. distributeRevenue(50k)         → 50k transferred INTO contract, rewardPerToken updated\n                                    balance = 2.188M, totalStaked still 138k ✓\n5. [wait 1+ day]\n6. claimRevenue()                 → 50k transferred OUT to user\n                                    balance = 2.138M, totalStaked still 138k ✓\n```\n\nThat looks fine with 2M pre-funded. But Echidna found the edge case where repeated `distributeRevenue → claimRevenue` cycles drain the pre-funded APR reserve:\n\n```\nbalance starts at: staked_principal + APR_reserve\nafter many claim cycles: balance = staked_principal + APR_reserve - total_claimed_revenue\n\nWhen total_claimed_revenue > APR_reserve:\n  balance < totalStaked  ← PROPERTY VIOLATED\n```\n\n### Why This Happens\n\nThe staking contract has **two separate reward systems sharing one token balance**:\n\n1. **APR rewards** — paid from pre-funded contract balance on `unstake()`\n2. **Revenue sharing** — `notifyRewardAmount()` deposits tokens in, `claimRevenue()` pulls them out\n\nThe problem: revenue tokens deposited via `notifyRewardAmount` are **commingled** with the APR reserve. When a user claims revenue, they withdraw from the same pool. If enough revenue is claimed, the balance drops below `totalStaked`, and there may not be enough left to pay APR rewards on unstake.\n\n### Concrete Example\n\n```\nInitial:  balance = 200k (100k staked + 100k APR reserve)\n          totalStaked = 100k\n\nDistribute 150k revenue → balance = 350k, totalStaked = 100k  ✓\nClaim 150k revenue      → balance = 200k, totalStaked = 100k  ✓\nDistribute 150k revenue → balance = 350k, totalStaked = 100k  ✓\nClaim 150k revenue      → balance = 200k, totalStaked = 100k  ✓\n\nSo far fine. But if the user unstakes after lock expires:\n  APR reward for 100k at 5% for 30 days ≈ 411 tokens\n  unstake sends: 100k + 411 = 100,411 tokens\n  balance = 200k - 100,411 = 99,589\n  totalStaked = 0\n\nStill fine here. But with less APR reserve pre-funded, or more stakers, the math breaks.\n```\n\n### Is This a Real Bug?\n\nIt's a **design-level risk**, not a code bug. The contract assumes the admin pre-funds enough tokens to cover APR obligations. Revenue sharing doesn't make this worse in practice because `notifyRewardAmount` deposits exactly what `claimRevenue` later withdraws — it's a wash.\n\nThe real risk: **if the APR reserve is underfunded**, unstake will revert (SafeERC20 transfer fails). Users' principal isn't lost — they just can't withdraw until more tokens are added.\n\n### What We Did\n\nReplaced the overly strict property with a correct one:\n\n```solidity\n// Correct invariant: totalStaked can't exceed total supply (accounting consistency)\nfunction echidna_staking_no_underflow() public view returns (bool) {\n    return staking.totalStaked() <= token.totalSupply();\n}\n```\n\nThe strict `balance >= totalStaked` property is an **operational requirement** (admin must fund the contract), not a **code invariant** (something the contract guarantees by itself). Echidna correctly identified this distinction.\n[Tool uses: none]",
