    "\n\nThe spec itself is inconsistent. It says two contradictory things:\n\n**Line 62:**\n> \"Lock-up Period: Duration options (30, 90, 180 days) with **different APY rates**\"\n\n**Line 104:**\n> \"Staking for Profit: Profit from warehouse rental (**not token inflation**) enters Staking Pool as reward\"\n\n\"Different APY rates\" implies a fixed, guaranteed return per tier — which requires pre-funded tokens (inflation). \"Not token inflation\" says the opposite — rewards only come from rent.\n\nBoth can't be true simultaneously. If rewards come purely from rent, there's no guaranteed APY — the yield depends on how much rent comes in. The \"different APY rates per tier\" becomes meaningless unless it means something else, like **different revenue share weights per tier** (e.g., tier 3 stakers get 2x the share of tier 0).\n\nRight now our implementation chose the \"not inflation\" side — all rewards from rent, unstake returns principal only. The lock tiers exist but only determine lock duration, not yield rate.\n\nThe open question: should longer lock tiers get a **higher share** of rental revenue? The spec hints at this (\"different APY rates\") but doesn't define the mechanism. Options:\n\n1. **Flat** — all stakers get equal share per token (current implementation)\n2. **Weighted** — tier multiplier on eligible stake (e.g., tier 0 = 1x, tier 1 = 1.5x, tier 2 = 2x, tier 3 = 3x)\n\nWhich do you want?\n[Tool uses: none]",
