    "> rescueTokens? what are those\"?",
    "\n\nIt's an admin function to recover tokens that get accidentally sent to a contract address.\n\nExample: someone copies the staking contract address and sends 1000 USDT to it directly (not through `stake()`). Those tokens are now stuck — the contract has no function to move them out. `rescueTokens` lets the admin withdraw them.\n\nThe marketplace already has it:\n\n```solidity\nfunction rescueTokens(address tokenAddr, uint256 amount) external onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {\n    IERC20(tokenAddr).safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount);\n}\n```\n\nThe spec mentions it on line 39: \"Emergency Withdraw: Admin function to recover tokens accidentally sent to contract.\"\n\nThe staking contract doesn't have one. If someone accidentally sends a random token (say USDT, WBNB, etc.) to the staking contract, it's permanently locked. The nuance is you'd want to prevent the admin from \"rescuing\" the actual staking token (RWA), since that would break solvency — so a proper implementation would either block rescue of the staking token, or cap it to `balance - totalStaked`.\n\nIt's a nice-to-have safety net, not critical. Up to you whether it's worth adding.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> resolveDispute explain function please",
    "\n\nIt's in `RWACertificate.sol`. When there's a dispute over a real-world asset (e.g., buyer claims the warehouse doesn't match the description), the admin freezes the NFT:\n\n```solidity\n// Step 1: Admin freezes the NFT\nfunction dispute(uint256 tokenId) external onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {\n    // Only Active or Redeemed NFTs can be disputed\n    asset.status = AssetStatus.Disputed;\n}\n```\n\nWhile `Disputed`, the NFT can't be transferred or traded — it's frozen.\n\nThen after investigation, the admin resolves it:\n\n```solidity\n// Step 2: Admin resolves — sets whatever status they want\nfunction resolveDispute(uint256 tokenId, AssetStatus newStatus) external onlyRole(DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE) {\n    // Must currently be Disputed\n    asset.status = newStatus;  // ← no validation on what newStatus is\n}\n```\n\nThe problem (TBR-17): `newStatus` is unchecked. Admin could set it to anything — `Active` (unfreeze, give back to owner), `Cancelled` (refund path), but also `Fulfilled`, `Expired`, or even `Disputed` again. There's no restriction on what's a valid resolution.\n\nSensible resolutions would be:\n- **Active** → dispute resolved in owner's favor, NFT unfrozen\n- **Cancelled** → dispute resolved against owner, NFT can be burned/refunded\n[Tool uses: none]",
